  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Wedding cakes, coal wagons and a coffin all arrived uninvited. One London address. One morning in 1810. Nobody inside had ordered anything.",
        "visual_query": "georgian london street terraced houses",
        "visual_fallback": "old european street"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Fifty-four Berners Street belonged to a Mrs Tottenham. Chimney sweeps knocked at five in the morning. Then bakers, furniture vans, an organ.",
        "visual_query": "19th century horse drawn cart",
        "visual_fallback": "horse drawn carriage"
      },
      {
        "narration": "By afternoon police had to block the street. The Duke of Gloucester turned up. So did the chairman of the Bank of England.",
        "visual_query": "regency london crowd street scene",
        "visual_fallback": "busy old street"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Who does this to a stranger? A twenty-two-year-old writer named Theodore Hook, for a one-guinea bet that he could make any address famous.",
        "visual_query": "antique quill letter writing desk",
        "visual_fallback": "old handwritten letters"
      },
      {
        "narration": "He had spent six weeks writing up to four thousand letters. Police offered a reward and never caught him. He confessed in a novel, twenty-five years later.",
        "visual_query": "english countryside village lane 1800s",
        "visual_fallback": "old leather book"
      }
    ],
